/* Mister-authorised 19 March 2031 Champions League Barcelona R16 comeback. In-world fictional journalism; factual match events only. */
(()=>{'use strict';const D=window.NAPOLI_DATA||(window.NAPOLI_DATA={});const items=[{"id":"napoli-barcelona-two-nil-moore-pio-estevao-r16-comeback-mar19-2031","category":"Match Report","date":"19 March 2031","byline":"Giulia Romano · Radio Napoli Centrale","headline":"FROM THE BRINK TO THE QUARTERFINALS: MOORE, PIO AND ESTÊVÃO TURN BARCELONA OVER","dek":"A first-touch Moore opener after three minutes levels the tie. Pio heads the winner at 75. Estêvão creates both as Napoli overturn a 2–1 first-leg deficit.","image":"assets/story-european-team-celebration.webp","imageCredit":"Approved fictional European celebration illustration; not a photograph of this Barcelona match.","body":["Barcelona arrived in Naples with a one-goal advantage and the comfort of knowing a draw would send them through. Three minutes later, that advantage had disappeared. By the seventy-fifth, Napoli had taken the tie away from them. And by the final whistle, a Champions League campaign that had been in danger in Catalonia was headed for the quarterfinals.","The score at the Maradona was Napoli 2, Barcelona 0. Across two legs it was Napoli 3, Barcelona 2. The numbers tell you who advanced. They do not quite capture the nerve required to do it.","Estêvão delivered the cross in the third minute, Mikey Moore met it with his first touch and Napoli had the start they needed. A side that had left Barcelona trailing 2–1 was level on aggregate almost before the visitors had settled into the rhythm of the return.","That opening goal had been made possible by another late one eight days earlier. In Spain, Endrick and Pedri had put Barcelona 2–0 ahead before Moore supplied Maximilian Beier at 81 minutes. The away defeat was painful; the Moore-to-Beier combination ensured the deficit was only one.","Moore's contribution to this tie therefore stretches across both nights. He gave Beier the opportunity to keep Napoli in it in Barcelona, then took his own chance immediately on home soil. First the provider, then the scorer: two interventions that changed what Barcelona were required to survive.","Napoli's early strike did not put them through. At 1–0 on the evening, the aggregate was 2–2. One Barcelona goal would have changed the equation again. The tension did not vanish with Moore's finish; it merely changed its shape.","That is what made the next major attacking moment so cruel. In the fifty-fourth minute, Francesco Pio Esposito struck the post. The chance to put Napoli ahead in the tie had presented itself, and the woodwork denied him.","Even in that moment the central question was not whether Pio had found the perfect finish. It was whether Napoli could create another opportunity before the night ran out. A team cannot afford to spend the remainder of a knockout match mourning a ball off the frame of the goal.","At seventy-five minutes, Pio answered. Estêvão supplied another cross; this time Pio rose to head it home. Napoli led 2–0 on the night and 3–2 on aggregate. The striker who had struck the post was now the man whose goal would send them through.","The two decisive deliveries were credited officially to the same player. Estêvão finished with two assists, not a general impression of influence or a hopeful claim of involvement. He provided the ball for both goals that eliminated Barcelona.","There was a selection story as well. Estêvão and Moore started, while Michael Olise and Nico Paz did not. Napoli got decisive contributions from both starters, with the former creating twice and the latter scoring the opener. The evidence of this match is unmistakable without needing to turn a single result into a verdict on every player not selected.","The other half of the victory should not be reduced to a footnote. Barcelona scored twice in the first leg, through Endrick at 22 minutes and Pedri at 51. In Naples they did not score at all. The home clean sheet was as necessary as Pio's winner.","Only three days earlier, Napoli had gone to Roma with a rotated squad and won 2–0. Neves and Beier scored, the league advantage grew to eleven points, and Napoli returned to their European assignment without the damage of a lost domestic confrontation.","This is how an extraordinary week becomes more than two unrelated wins. Napoli beat their nearest league pursuers away, then eliminated Barcelona after losing the first leg. The club remains alive in Europe while leading Serie A on 74 points from 29 matches.","Do not mistake qualification for a guarantee of what comes next. The quarterfinal opponent and its dates will be settled in due course. But a club's right to imagine those nights has to be earned on the pitch, and Napoli earned it.","At the start of the tie, Barcelona had a lead. At the start of the second leg, they still had it. Three minutes into the return, Moore erased it. Seventy-five minutes in, Pio overturned it. Estêvão supplied the ball for both. The final whistle supplied the only verdict that mattered: Napoli are through."],"comments":[{"user":"CurvaB","text":"THREE MINUTES. Moore gave us air. Seventy-five minutes. Pio gave us the quarterfinals. I am not sleeping tonight.","replies":[{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"I spent seventy-two minutes between those goals unable to sit properly.","replies":[]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"At least you managed to sit at all. I wore a path through the living room.","replies":[]},{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"A proper European night. The sort you remember long after the league table changes.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"His assist in Barcelona kept this tie within reach. His goal in Naples made the comeback possible. Please remember BOTH legs.","replies":[{"user":"BeierHive","text":"And Max finishing that pass in Spain deserves its place in the story.","replies":[]},{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"Absolutely. It was a two-leg rescue, not a single-night miracle.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"PioNation","text":"He hit the post at 54 and returned to head the winner at 75. That's the part I'll remember about Pio.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Good strikers do not let an earlier chance determine their next one.","replies":[]},{"user":"PioNation","text":"Exactly. He had every reason to be frustrated and still found the goal.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"Remember when Pedri made it 2–0 in Barcelona and it looked grim? That Beier goal from Moore in the first leg became huge.","replies":[{"user":"CurvaCalculator","text":"First leg 1–2, second leg 2–0. Aggregate 3–2. Every Napoli goal has a job in that story.","replies":[]},{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"Don't make me do maths right now. I'm celebrating.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"Excellent result, but allowing Pio to hit the post at 54 was an unnecessary use of woodwork. Unacceptable resource management.","replies":[{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"We just knocked Barcelona out.","replies":[]},{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"And how much did that post contribute? Zero goals.","replies":[]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"The post has requested a transfer away from you.","replies":[]},{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"I would negotiate very hard.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"Barcelona supporter here. A lead after the first leg, gone after three minutes in Naples. Hurts to go out without scoring in the return.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"Endrick and Pedri made us suffer in Spain. It was never a comfortable tie from our side.","replies":[]},{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"Fair. Pio's header made the difference, but the early goal changed the whole mood.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Two goals make the headlines; preventing Barcelona from scoring in Naples made those goals enough.","replies":[{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"That clean sheet is a European result in itself.","replies":[]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Exactly. If they get one, the calculations change again.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"NevesAppreciation","text":"Neves scores against Roma, then the boys finish the Barcelona comeback. A fairly outrageous four days.","replies":[{"user":"SerieAWatcher","text":"And we're still eleven clear with nine league games left. We cannot lose sight of that.","replies":[]},{"user":"NevesAppreciation","text":"No one is forgetting Roma. Tonight just happens to belong to Europe.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"The most beautiful bit is that the winner came twenty-one minutes after Pio hit the post. Football offers a second sentence if you stay in the story.","replies":[]},{"user":"BarcaPerspective","text":"Blaugrana fan. We were ahead in Barcelona, but a second leg is ninety more minutes of football. Napoli earned it.","replies":[{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"Hard to argue otherwise. Two crosses, two goals, and we had no answer on the scoreboard.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BeierHive","text":"Max doesn't score tonight and is STILL central to why we're going through. That 81st-minute first-leg goal means so much now.","replies":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"Moore assisted it. We are both eating very well tonight.","replies":[]}]}]},{"id":"estevao-two-assists-moore-pio-barcelona-mar19-2031","category":"Player Focus","date":"20 March 2031","byline":"Elena De Luca · Player desk","headline":"TWO CROSSES. TWO ASSISTS. ESTÊVÃO AND MOORE HAVE CHANGED NAPOLI'S EUROPEAN STORY","dek":"The Brazilian created both goals against Barcelona. Moore opened the scoring after first setting up Napoli's vital first-leg away goal.","image":"assets/story-coaches-key-players-walkout.webp","imageCredit":"Approved fictional Napoli coaching-and-players illustration; not documentary footage from the match.","body":["One cross can alter a match. Two crosses can alter a Champions League tie. Estêvão delivered both of Napoli's decisive assists against Barcelona, the first after three minutes and the second at seventy-five, and made the strongest possible statement without scoring himself.","The temptation after a match like this is to invent an argument that one attacker has permanently replaced another. Football rarely stays that simple. What Napoli do have is proof that their attacking options can change the course of the biggest fixture on the calendar.","Estêvão started the home leg. So did Mikey Moore. Michael Olise and Nico Paz did not start. That selection was followed by Moore finishing Estêvão's early cross first time and Pio Esposito heading home another delivery from the Brazilian in the second half.","Three minutes into a tie that Barcelona led 2–1, Moore had wiped out the deficit. The finish deserves the praise it has received, but the ball had to arrive in a position where a single touch was enough. Estêvão made that possible.","His second assist was not simply a repeat for the sake of repetition. By the seventy-fifth minute, the contest had been level on aggregate for most of the evening and Pio had already hit the post at 54. Another opportunity was needed, and Estêvão delivered one.","Pio supplied the header. It is a decisive striker's contribution, but also a reminder that timing and service remain part of the craft. Napoli had their finisher in position and their creator willing to find him again.","Moore's role is larger still when the first leg is included. In Barcelona he assisted Beier's goal at 81, reducing the deficit from two goals to one. In Naples he converted the first chance of the return into the equalising aggregate score.","That sequence says something about a player still forming his career: the same opponent demanded two different kinds of decisive intervention, and Moore provided both. The assist in defeat and the goal in victory should be discussed together.","Against Roma three days before the return, Estêvão had also created João Neves's opener in a 2–0 away win. That was a different competition and a different kind of match, but the same emerging thread: the Brazilian is influencing important games through his final ball.","The tracked club-season contribution now stands at two goals and five assists for Estêvão, with both Barcelona assists added. Moore's corresponding tally is eight goals and nine assists. Those figures are useful context, but this Champions League tie is a stronger argument for their immediate importance than any raw total.","Olise and Paz remain significant figures in a squad fighting across several competitions. The question raised by the Barcelona result is not how to erase established talent from the conversation. It is how to accommodate the production of two young attackers whose big-match performances demand serious attention.","Napoli have moved past Barcelona with a clean sheet, two goals and two players who supplied the decisive connection twice. That is a selection dilemma any ambitious squad should welcome.","Nothing about this guarantees another perfect European night. It does make Estêvão and Moore more than promising alternatives. Against Barcelona, their partnership became an answer."],"comments":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"If this article leads to the words 'he's still just a prospect' I might throw my phone.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"A prospect can also be decisive. Both can be true.","replies":[]},{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"Then write 'decisive prospect' in enormous letters.","replies":[]},{"user":"NevesAppreciation","text":"That seems a fair compromise.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"Moore gets the third-minute goal, Estêvão gets the two assists. They were not passengers in this comeback.","replies":[{"user":"PioNation","text":"And Pio still had to head the second one in. Creators and finisher.","replies":[]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"No argument. Everybody gets a slice tonight.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"One spectacular match doesn't make Olise a bad player. I want the entire attack available for the run-in.","replies":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"Agreed about Olise. But Estêvão shouldn't disappear after a performance like that.","replies":[]},{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"That's exactly my position. Competition, not a purge.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"The most important part of the two crosses is how quickly Moore finished the first and how Pio attacked the second. Service means nothing without the movement.","replies":[{"user":"PioNation","text":"People talk about 'the cross' as if the striker can just stand still and wait.","replies":[]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Quite. The partnership is the story, not one man's highlight reel.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"Two assists from Estêvão, yet he somehow failed to score either goal. We need to question his selfishness in refusing the limelight.","replies":[{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"Assists are literally the opposite of selfish.","replies":[]},{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"That is why the situation is so complex.","replies":[]},{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"You should be studied at a university.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"The Roma assist and then two against Barça. Whatever happens next, that is a week he'll be proud of.","replies":[{"user":"NevesAppreciation","text":"Neves will surely appreciate the Roma one too. The timing was beautiful.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"Keep Olise, Paz, Moore and Estêvão hungry and this club has a lovely problem.","replies":[]},{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"From the other side, it was the repeated service that hurt. You see one cross become a goal and then the second one decides the tie.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Good opposition analysis. We felt exactly how dangerous those deliveries could be.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"PioNation","text":"38 tracked Napoli club goals for Pio now. But I don't care about the number nearly as much as what the latest one did.","replies":[{"user":"BeierHive","text":"I feel the same about Max's first-leg goal. The weight of the goal matters.","replies":[]}]}]},{"id":"bellandi-napoli-barcelona-quarterfinal-after-comeback-mar19-2031","category":"Opinion","date":"20 March 2031","byline":"Matteo Bellandi · Linea Calcio","headline":"BELLANDI: NAPOLI HAVE EARNED THEIR QUARTERFINAL. NOW STOP CONFUSING BELIEF WITH A PLAN","dek":"Two starts, two decisive creators, one clean sheet. Barcelona are gone, but the questions of Europe will only become sharper.","image":"assets/story-champions-league-stakes.webp","imageCredit":"Approved fictional Champions League stakes illustration; not a photograph of the second leg.","body":["Congratulations are due. Napoli lost 2–1 in Barcelona, went home and won 2–0. They overturned the tie 3–2 on aggregate, scored twice through Mikey Moore and Pio Esposito, and kept a clean sheet against a team that had hurt them twice in Spain. That is not luck disguised as resilience. It is the work required to survive a knockout tie.","Now that I have said the nice things, we can talk about what matters next.","The third-minute opener was a gift to every tactical plan that begins with the words 'score early'. Estêvão crossed, Moore finished first time, and the visitors' advantage was gone. There is no sensible way to pretend the starting selections did not pay off.","The other important detail is that Napoli still needed a second goal to advance. At 1–0 they were level, not through. Pio striking the post at 54 provided a reminder that European football does not always give you the finish you believe you deserve.","At 75, Pio made his second chance count. Estêvão supplied the cross and the striker headed Napoli into the quarterfinals. Two deliveries from the same player, two different finishers, and the second was the winning intervention across the tie.","Some will declare Estêvão the permanent answer to every attacking question. That is a mistake. Others will be so protective of the existing order that they pretend two official assists against Barcelona prove nothing. That is a different mistake, and perhaps a more stubborn one.","The sensible conclusion is that Napoli now possess a genuine argument for keeping Estêvão heavily involved. Moore has a similarly persuasive case: he assisted Beier's late goal in Spain and scored the opener in Naples. Europe has a way of forcing opportunities into the hands of those who use them.","Remember, too, the side that defeated Roma 2–0 between these European legs. A rotated squad kept a clean sheet away to second place in Serie A, moved Napoli eleven points clear and made it possible to approach the Barcelona return without a fresh league disaster to carry.","That is an extraordinary achievement of squad management across one week. But qualification does not guarantee that every selection, every defensive decision and every approach to a future opponent is already correct.","The quarterfinals will offer a new assignment. It is not necessary to name an opponent before one is set, nor to describe a victory that has not happened. Napoli must be ready for a different match rather than attempting to recreate the third-minute cross as a system.","I have criticised this team's tendency to invite pressure in big matches, and I will continue to do so when they warrant it. Here is the counterargument to my own criticism: Barcelona could not score in Naples. That is the substance of a serious European performance.","Napoli's supporters are entitled to enjoy a famous comeback. The players are entitled to hear what they have accomplished. Everyone else at the club must make sure the next round begins with clear eyes.","Dream, by all means. Napoli have earned the right. But the teams who go deepest in this competition are the ones capable of waking up tomorrow and planning properly."],"comments":[{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"Bellandi has finally discovered the word 'congratulations'. Somebody save the recording.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Read the rest. His concern about planning the next tie is sound.","replies":[]},{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"I did. I am permitted one evening of enjoying his discomfort.","replies":[]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"You may have two.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"He's right that winning the tie isn't the same as winning the next round. He's also right that tonight deserves celebration.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"A rare balanced Bellandi column. I'm suspicious.","replies":[]},{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"Even a critic can recognise a result when it is two goals and a clean sheet.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"I am absolutely using 'Europe forces opportunities into the hands of those who use them' in every Moore argument.","replies":[{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"Then remember the part about not making permanent judgments from one match.","replies":[]},{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"Fine. I'll use the whole paragraph.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"Bellandi is finally asking the correct question. Why didn't Napoli score in minute one instead of minute three?","replies":[{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"Please don't associate us with this line of reasoning.","replies":[]},{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"Two minutes lost. A historic inefficiency.","replies":[]},{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"The manager could win the trophy and you'd ask about the trophy's packaging.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"From a Barça supporter: Napoli deserved the passage. I don't think this is the night for lectures.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Perhaps. But a column written the next day has a different job from the match report.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"CurvaCalculator","text":"The numbers I care about: 2–0 on the night, 3–2 over two legs, 74 points in the league. Different competitions, different jobs.","replies":[{"user":"SerieAWatcher","text":"Yes. And the league lead doesn't get bigger because of a European win.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BeierHive","text":"The column should mention that Beier's 81st-minute goal in Spain made this possible. That's the hinge.","replies":[{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"It does in the other coverage, rightly. We got to the return with a one-goal deficit instead of two.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"Bellandi can have his planning. Tonight belongs to the lads who turned the tie around.","replies":[]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"I like this distinction: belief gives you courage, organisation gives that courage direction.","replies":[{"user":"CurvaB","text":"I'm saving that for the quarterfinal. Tonight I'm still shouting.","replies":[]}]}]}];const ids=new Set(items.map(x=>x.id));D.articles=[...items,...(D.articles||[]).filter(a=>!ids.has(a.id))];})();

/* Barcelona comeback reader expansion 20 March 2031 — retain existing stories and discussions. */
(()=>{'use strict';const D=window.NAPOLI_DATA||(window.NAPOLI_DATA={});const additional={"napoli-barcelona-two-nil-moore-pio-estevao-r16-comeback-mar19-2031":[{"user":"VesuvioDad","text":"Moore scored before my coffee was ready. My son grabbed me and we knocked the whole cup over. The coffee can stay there until tomorrow.","replies":[{"user":"CurvaB","text":"That is your son's first lesson in European football: furniture is temporary.","replies":[{"user":"VesuvioDad","text":"He was born after our first Scudetto. He thinks nights like this happen to everyone.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"Don't hurry to correct him. Let him enjoy what we've built.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"The coffee deserves an assist for setting the mood.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"PartenopeNorth","text":"Watching in Toronto. At minute three I woke the whole house. At minute seventy-five I woke everybody who'd somehow fallen asleep again.","replies":[{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"A Napoli fan is keeping a neighbourhood awake somewhere in every time zone.","replies":[{"user":"PartenopeNorth","text":"My apology to the neighbours will read: Francesco Pio Esposito, 75th minute.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"Barcelona fans in Toronto are awake too. For much less enjoyable reasons.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"PioNation","text":"At 54 I thought that post would haunt us until August. At 75 Pio headed the winner and my brain simply stopped working.","replies":[{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"I was already rehearsing how to explain the cruelty of football.","replies":[{"user":"PioNation","text":"Then he scored and I forgot every word of the speech.","replies":[{"user":"CurvaB","text":"The new speech is just screaming until you cry.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"A striker's great response to one missed chance is remaining ready for the next delivery.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"We lost to PSG in 2028, beat Real Madrid in 2029, beat Porto in 2030 and now turn around Barcelona. Young supporters cannot imagine what that journey feels like.","replies":[{"user":"VesuvioDad","text":"I remember sitting in silence after PSG. I wondered if we'd ever get back to a final.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"And now we're irritated by a one-goal first-leg loss as defending champions. Imagine telling us that then.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"The PSG scar is why I refused to surrender the tie when Pio hit the woodwork.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"We spent eight days holding a lead and it lasted THREE MINUTES in Naples. That is the part I cannot get past.","replies":[{"user":"BarcaPerspective","text":"Worse, we had seventy-two minutes after the leveller to answer before Pio scored.","replies":[{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"We were still in it. That's what hurts. We couldn't find the one goal.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"The opener levelled the tie, not settled it. That is why the waiting mattered.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"Somebody understand me: from Moore in the THIRD minute to Pio in the SEVENTY-FIFTH we were level on aggregate. Then that header. I screamed before I knew I had lungs.","replies":[{"user":"PioNation","text":"I made a noise I have never made before.","replies":[{"user":"CurvaB","text":"Mine was a broken kettle having a nervous breakdown.","replies":[{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"I am sending Pio the bill for what happened to my nervous system.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"And both goals came from Estêvão crosses. What a night for those lads.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BeierHive","text":"Everyone celebrating tonight must remember Beier's 81st-minute goal in Barcelona. Without it these two goals wouldn't have been enough.","replies":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"And Moore assisted Max in Spain before scoring in Naples. This was a two-leg rescue.","replies":[{"user":"BeierHive","text":"Precisely. I won't let the first leg disappear because the second was beautiful.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"PioNation","text":"Three goals, three pieces of one comeback. There's room for everyone's flowers.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"At 1–0 I couldn't sit. At 2–0 I couldn't breathe. I have concluded that football is an unreasonable hobby.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"You forgot looking at the clock every minute and discovering it moves backwards.","replies":[{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"I swear the clock returned to 74 after Pio scored.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"CurvaCalculator","text":"I was calculating seventeen different ways it could still go wrong.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"JuveWatch","text":"Juventus fan here. I resent every sentence I am about to write, but Napoli handled that European return brilliantly.","replies":[{"user":"CurvaB","text":"We accept this compliment with extreme suspicion.","replies":[{"user":"JuveWatch","text":"Do not mistake it for affection.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"Rival fans praising our football? Clear evidence the manager has lowered standards.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Two goals take the headlines, but the clean sheet gave Pio's header the power to win the aggregate. People are treating the defence like a footnote.","replies":[{"user":"BarcaPerspective","text":"As a Barcelona fan, that is the part I dread admitting. We had a whole match and never found the goal.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Against Barcelona, preventing that goal is an achievement of its own.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BeierHive","text":"Agreed. This was an entire team's qualification.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"Assist in Barcelona. Goal in Naples. Against Barcelona. If someone calls Mikey a passenger again, at least spell his name correctly.","replies":[{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"He's earned a big role. That doesn't require us to insult Olise.","replies":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"I never asked for that. I'm asking for a real competition for places.","replies":[{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"Then we're on the same side. The next selection is fascinating.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"PioNation","text":"The first goal gave my striker the chance to score the winner. I'm grateful for both.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"Moore required three whole minutes to score and Pio needed seventy-five. Disgraceful inefficiency against an opponent gracious enough to allow ninety.","replies":[{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"Please experience joy at least once in your life.","replies":[{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"I am experiencing concern about the seventy-four squandered minutes.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"You would criticise sunrise for being late.","replies":[{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"Finally somebody understands the standards.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"Three consecutive Scudetti, two Champions Leagues, a treble, and now this. Let me celebrate before we start making predictions about the quarterfinal.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"The danger of success is acting as if it ever becomes ordinary.","replies":[{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"My hands are still shaking. It isn't ordinary in this house.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"PartenopeNorth","text":"Everyone in my family knows exactly what happened at 75 tonight.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BarcaPerspective","text":"From the Barcelona side: we're furious, but this wasn't stolen from us. We had the advantage and Napoli earned the comeback.","replies":[{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"There is no comfort in pretending two goals and a clean sheet happened by accident.","replies":[{"user":"BarcaPerspective","text":"It would only make the exit feel even worse.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"A generous reaction in defeat. Respect.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"CurvaCalculator","text":"Moore at three made the AGGREGATE LEVEL. Pio at seventy-five made the AGGREGATE NAPOLI 3–2 BARCELONA. I've seen too many people say we qualified at minute three.","replies":[{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"You expect arithmetic from people who had just spent seventy-two minutes terrified?","replies":[{"user":"CurvaCalculator","text":"I had the calculations on paper.","replies":[{"user":"CurvaB","text":"Do not bring your notebook to the quarterfinal.","replies":[]}]}]}]},{"user":"NevesAppreciation","text":"Rotated victory at Roma, eleven clear in Serie A, then this against Barcelona. Different players stepped up on two enormous nights.","replies":[{"user":"BeierHive","text":"Neves and Max at Roma, then Moore, Pio and Estêvão tonight. That's what squad depth means.","replies":[{"user":"NevesAppreciation","text":"And it's why the league position didn't have to be sacrificed to recover in Europe.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"SerieAWatcher","text":"Enjoy it. But Lazio won't give us points for eliminating Barcelona.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"VesuvioDad","text":"My son asked if Pio knew everybody was screaming when he headed that cross in. I told him Pio probably had a slightly different priority.","replies":[{"user":"PioNation","text":"Imagine being the one under that ball with a quarterfinal on the line. My knees wobble thinking about it.","replies":[]},{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"Children ask more intelligent questions than half the television panels.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"MilanRossonero","text":"Milan fan. Napoli have become maddeningly good at turning adversity into the next headline.","replies":[{"user":"JuveWatch","text":"I hate that I recognise the feeling.","replies":[{"user":"MilanRossonero","text":"I hate that we're agreeing.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"This thread of shared rival suffering is almost as lovely as the winner.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"There was a moment after Pio's header when the score changed and my brain refused to believe it had actually happened.","replies":[{"user":"VesuvioDad","text":"I stared at the screen as if they could still take the goal away.","replies":[{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"I was genuinely afraid to blink.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"That's the emotion that won't show up in a season statistics page.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"PartenopeNorth","text":"PSG in 2028 left me motionless. Madrid in 2029 had me jumping. Porto in 2030 had me crying. Pio's header put me on the floor.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"From heartbreak to two European titles, then another comeback. It's extraordinary.","replies":[{"user":"PartenopeNorth","text":"Extraordinary and somehow I still cannot stay calm for ninety minutes.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Choosing Moore and Estêvão for a knockout comeback and receiving the opener plus both assists is management worth crediting. It does not mean Olise or Paz are finished.","replies":[{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"Thank you. Estêvão can be magnificent without Olise suddenly being worthless.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Competition should make a strong team stronger.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"Exactly, provided we stop treating the youngsters as emergency decorations.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"A great victory doesn't mean every decision was perfect. It does mean we're allowed to cry over a famous comeback before discussing the next flaw.","replies":[{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"I am still in the crying phase.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"Good. Stay there a little longer.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"Stop saying Barcelona merely ran out of time. We had the lead, the tie was level almost all night, and we couldn't answer. Napoli took the opportunity.","replies":[{"user":"BarcaPerspective","text":"The best teams punish you if your reply never comes.","replies":[{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"Tonight we found that out twice.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"PioNation","text":"I'll remember that header more clearly than most birthdays. He hit the post, found another chance and sent us through.","replies":[{"user":"BeierHive","text":"You're entitled to be impossible to live with for at least a week.","replies":[{"user":"PioNation","text":"I was planning on being impossible anyway.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"I will remember the alarming period during which the ball was still in the air.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"Tomorrow we can discuss Lazio and Milan and the quarterfinal. Tonight I'm phoning everybody I love to shout WE'RE THROUGH down the line.","replies":[{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"I've done it three times. Two people hung up because they already knew.","replies":[{"user":"CurvaB","text":"They can hear it again.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"VesuvioDad","text":"My son says he wants to watch every game with me now. That alone was worth the nerves.","replies":[]}]}],"estevao-two-assists-moore-pio-barcelona-mar19-2031":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"I keep seeing Mikey meet the third-minute cross first time. At that stage of a European knockout tie, there isn't an easier finish to imagine and he didn't take an extra touch.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"The early delivery was useful because the finish needed no delay.","replies":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"And the nerve of that first touch against Barcelona is what I'm celebrating.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"PioNation","text":"The pass and the finish belonged to the same beautiful moment.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"I love Olise and I'm thrilled for Estêvão. Please stop acting as if supporting one is an insult to the other.","replies":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"Would you start Olise over him in the next quarterfinal?","replies":[{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"I don't know. The next opponent matters. That's why having both is such an advantage.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"An actual selection discussion at last. Context, not a loyalty test.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"What matters is that Estêvão has earned a serious claim, not a ceremonial pat on the back.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"EstadaoNapoli","text":"Brazilian supporter here. Two assists against Barcelona in a Champions League comeback is exactly the sort of night you dream of for a young creator.","replies":[{"user":"PartenopeNorth","text":"The assist at Roma as well. He's had an extraordinary week.","replies":[{"user":"EstadaoNapoli","text":"What impresses me is that these passes mattered when the matches weren't comfortable.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"From the other side he was the problem that kept coming back. Twice.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"PioNation","text":"Everyone talks about Estêvão twice assisting. Rightly. But Pio still had to attack that second ball after he'd been denied by the post.","replies":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"And Mikey's opener made that header the tie winner, not merely the leveller.","replies":[{"user":"PioNation","text":"I know. I screamed for them both.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"A cross is an invitation. The finisher still has to arrive.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"The most interesting thing about these assists is that the same creator found two different finishers at two different stages. That's genuine attacking variety.","replies":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"Both finishers committed immediately. That's why those deliveries worked.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Exactly. Don't turn an interplay of three people into a single-man highlight.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"And don't forget having alternatives makes it harder to plan against Napoli.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BeierHive","text":"Moore set up Beier in Spain. I refuse to let that 81st-minute assist vanish because the second leg was so dramatic.","replies":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"It won't. The away goal kept the door open and Mikey kicked it off the hinges at home.","replies":[{"user":"BeierHive","text":"Perfect. Max still deserves his share of the celebration.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"The first-leg goal made all of this mathematically possible.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"Every time I think about the second Estêvão cross I get a physical sensation of panic and joy. Is this a medical condition?","replies":[{"user":"PioNation","text":"It's supporting Napoli.","replies":[{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"Is the treatment watching more Napoli?","replies":[]}]},{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"The treatment appears to make it worse. None of us will stop.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BarcaPerspective","text":"Barcelona supporter. I can understand why your fans want Moore and Estêvão in the eleven again. They changed the whole tie.","replies":[{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"A good squad can make different choices next time without undoing what happened tonight.","replies":[{"user":"BarcaPerspective","text":"Your selection debate is an enviable problem compared with our elimination.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"When I was younger, a breakout European night was something we prayed for. Now Moore and Estêvão are writing their own chapters alongside the established winners.","replies":[{"user":"VesuvioDad","text":"My son thinks you just start young players against Barcelona and they automatically become legends.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"Teach him to enjoy the moment without expecting every match to be this kind.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"NevesAppreciation","text":"Estêvão created Neves's goal at Roma and both goals here. That week shows his final ball matters outside one special European evening.","replies":[{"user":"SerieAWatcher","text":"We need that contribution across competitions if we're going to finish the season strongly.","replies":[{"user":"NevesAppreciation","text":"Exactly. This isn't about picking one magical eleven for one night.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"Estêvão contributed two assists and failed to score either goal himself. A shocking refusal to seize the limelight.","replies":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"I cannot tell whether this is satire or a request for medical attention.","replies":[{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"I'm also concerned Moore scored in only one of the two legs.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BeierHive","text":"Max scored in the other leg. How many men did you expect in the same net?","replies":[]}]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"The next team sheet must be a genuine discussion. Not because Olise and Paz are poor players, but because Moore and Estêvão have made it impossible to treat those places as inherited.","replies":[{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"As long as a different selection next week doesn't become an insult to tonight's heroes.","replies":[{"user":"CurvaB","text":"Fine. But going back automatically would be an insult to the performance.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Selection is not a lifetime medal. It answers the next game's needs.","replies":[]}]}]}]},{"user":"PartenopeNorth","text":"My nephew spent the morning practicing Estêvão crosses in the living room instead of shots. The creator has become a hero too.","replies":[{"user":"EstadaoNapoli","text":"That makes me happier than any individual statistic.","replies":[{"user":"PartenopeNorth","text":"My lamp disagrees. The sofa was apparently playing Pio.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"We lost the first leg and two young attackers played a huge role in bringing us back. This is how a dynasty gets younger without getting smaller.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"That's the important part. They aren't waiting for the veterans to finish writing the story.","replies":[]},{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"Mikey has been pushing into the story for months. Nobody can call it a cameo now.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"I'm a Barcelona supporter and the repeated deliveries sting more than anything. We conceded to one cross and then to the same provider again when it mattered most.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Which is why credit belongs to the movement of the finishers as well as the delivery.","replies":[{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"I am not going to enjoy hearing the word cross for some time.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"PioNation","text":"The 54th-minute post gives the 75th-minute header a different feeling. It's a player being denied and refusing to let the chance define him.","replies":[{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"That's why I cried. Watching somebody receive another chance after the first one hurt.","replies":[{"user":"PioNation","text":"Football sometimes gives you the second chance while your hands are still shaking.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Don't use this comeback as an argument that we should discard attackers. It demonstrated the value of choice, not the value of having fewer choices.","replies":[{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"That's all I ask. Keep competition healthy.","replies":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"Agreed, so long as the competition really is open.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"VesuvioDad","text":"My son asked how one player could provide the balls for both goals that changed a knockout tie. I told him to watch the two crosses again.","replies":[{"user":"EstadaoNapoli","text":"He'll see a creator who trusted two teammates.","replies":[{"user":"VesuvioDad","text":"And a father trying very hard not to start crying again.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"MilanRossonero","text":"Even as a rival, it's a pleasure to watch the sort of young attacker who sees a pass that changes the match instead of forcing his own shot.","replies":[{"user":"JuveWatch","text":"You had me until the word pleasure. I find all of this intolerable.","replies":[{"user":"MilanRossonero","text":"That's the closest we'll get to agreeing.","replies":[]}]}]}],"bellandi-napoli-barcelona-quarterfinal-after-comeback-mar19-2031":[{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"Bellandi has found another pressure problem. TEN TROPHIES. THREE SCUDETTI. TWO EUROPEAN CUPS. At what point does this evidence enter his office?","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"A poor individual performance can be criticised. A sweeping claim about mentality should reckon with the silverware.","replies":[{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"Exactly. Discuss a defensive issue, fine. Don't pretend the last three years are gossip.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"And don't ask people to be embarrassed by a famous European comeback.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"Only ten trophies? The manager has had years to win eleven. Troubling.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"I hadn't finished celebrating Pio's header before Bellandi began assigning homework. Sometimes the city needs one night to be happy.","replies":[{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"A next-day columnist can analyse without forgetting what the previous night meant.","replies":[{"user":"CurvaB","text":"It was a comeback against Barcelona. Not a routine Wednesday.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"The best criticism starts from what actually happened.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"Critics said PSG in 2028 proved the final was too big. Then came Madrid, Porto and this Barcelona comeback. If every answer moves the question, perhaps the old argument is wrong.","replies":[{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"I kept those old columns. Funny how every prediction eventually becomes 'questions remain'.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"Questions may remain. The answers already supplied on the pitch should count.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"Past success isn't immunity. It's still evidence.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Bellandi's narrow point about planning for the next opponent is sensible. The headline makes it sound as if Napoli survived on luck and prayer.","replies":[{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"I can accept that distinction. It's his sweeping verdict that irritates me.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Then challenge that verdict, not his right to criticise.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"I can challenge it while celebrating.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"From Barcelona: I cannot understand a pressure accusation after Napoli overturned our first-leg lead and kept a clean sheet.","replies":[{"user":"BarcaPerspective","text":"We had from the third minute until the 75th to find an answer before the winner. It never came.","replies":[{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"I'll swap our 'lack of pressure issues' for their comeback without hesitation.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"The team we eliminated is making more sense than the columnist.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"I support Bellandi. Ten trophies prove nothing except ten separate opportunities to spend too much on silver polish.","replies":[{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"You'd call an unbeaten season a worrying failure to experience defeat.","replies":[{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"Correct. It demonstrates a lack of adversity exposure.","replies":[{"user":"CurvaB","text":"And this is why nobody lets you near the tactics board.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Please don't confuse an actual tactical argument with whatever that is.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"Three league titles, two consecutive European crowns, and now another quarterfinal. Pressure is beginning to look like the thing that makes this group dangerous.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"League dominance is pressure too. Every rival has thirty-eight chances to take a shot at you.","replies":[{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"And after twenty-nine matches this season we're eleven points clear.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"MilanRossonero","text":"Milan fan here. I hate Napoli's dominance, but claiming this side cannot handle major occasions is becoming absurd.","replies":[{"user":"JuveWatch","text":"Juventus supporter reluctantly agreeing with a Milan supporter. That's how badly Bellandi has read the room.","replies":[{"user":"MilanRossonero","text":"Don't tell anyone we agreed.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"Two rivals forming a coalition against your argument is a difficult afternoon.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"PioNation","text":"Pio hit the post at 54 and headed the winning goal at 75. How does that become proof of a team unsure of itself?","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"The column acknowledges it. Its best point is about the next tie.","replies":[{"user":"PioNation","text":"Then write a column about next-round preparation instead of questioning this team's nerve.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"That would be a more defensible headline.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"Sometimes the correct tactical insight is simply that the striker was magnificent.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"Bellandi says belief is not a plan while admitting Moore and Estêvão both started and both decided the result. Choosing them was a plan, wasn't it?","replies":[{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"A decision with rather spectacular evidence in its favour.","replies":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"And the manager deserves credit for making it.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"A plan isn't a guarantee. Its successful execution deserves recognition.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"SerieAWatcher","text":"It's bizarre to discuss our European mentality and barely engage with a 74-point league season. These players repeatedly handle expectation in different competitions.","replies":[{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"Roma away with a rotated group, then Barcelona at home. Two completely different tests.","replies":[{"user":"SerieAWatcher","text":"Both answered. In the space of three days.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"PartenopeNorth","text":"I woke up after one of the most emotional football nights of my life and found this article asking if we were properly prepared to feel joy.","replies":[{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"I woke up smiling. Bellandi woke up carrying a clipboard.","replies":[{"user":"PartenopeNorth","text":"I hope he at least bought a blue clipboard.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"Somebody find out whether he's ever smiled at a goal.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BeierHive","text":"Beier's goal at 81 in Barcelona made the second leg salvageable. Where does that act of resilience fit into this grand theory of weakness?","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"The comeback didn't begin when Moore scored at home. It began when Beier refused to let the first leg finish 2–0.","replies":[{"user":"BeierHive","text":"Exactly. A tie is two games, not one convenient headline.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Defence of the columnist: preparing for a quarterfinal is not identical to surviving a round of sixteen. A club can excel at the second and still need different ideas for the first.","replies":[{"user":"CurvaB","text":"Nobody here objects to new ideas. We object to being told the players haven't proved they can bear the biggest moments.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"Then we're precise: the psychological accusation is weak; the warning about tactics isn't.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"I agree with that. I still won't forgive the headline.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"VesuvioDad","text":"My son read the headline and asked why the man was upset when Napoli won. I didn't have a sensible answer for a child.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"Tell him football writers need to fill tomorrow's pages too.","replies":[{"user":"VesuvioDad","text":"He suggested Pio should write the reply with his forehead.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"NapoliTherapy","text":"The child has already improved the analysis.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"BarcaPerspective","text":"If I'm a Barcelona supporter seeking comfort in Bellandi's doubts, I'm disappointed. Napoli deserved the tie. There's no sense pretending otherwise.","replies":[{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"We were knocked out. Pretending the opposition didn't earn it would be dishonest.","replies":[{"user":"BarcaPerspective","text":"Let their next opponent assess them. We're out.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"CurvaCalculator","text":"Three Serie A, two Champions League, two Coppa Italia, two UEFA Super Cups, one Supercoppa. That's TEN. I have brought Bellandi the sums because apparently he left his calculator at home.","replies":[{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"Please mail the equation in enormous font.","replies":[{"user":"CurvaCalculator","text":"I considered a plaque.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"The calculator doesn't show how few goals we scored between minutes four and seventy-four.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"NevesAppreciation","text":"The 'planning' argument ignores how a rotated team won away to Roma so the club could approach Barcelona properly. That's a plan across competitions.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"He acknowledges Roma. I wish he'd developed that instead of returning to an old anxiety about mentality.","replies":[{"user":"NevesAppreciation","text":"That would have been a more useful critique.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"I will give Bellandi credit for discussing the selection dilemma without pretending Olise and Paz are suddenly useless.","replies":[{"user":"MooreMinutes","text":"One good paragraph. The rest sounds like he's reserving the right to predict disaster forever.","replies":[{"user":"OliseBeliever","text":"A critic should be allowed to say he underestimated a side without abandoning criticism.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"I WANT a difficult football critic. I just want him to react when reality changes, instead of carrying the same complaint into every season.","replies":[{"user":"TacticalNonno","text":"That's the best argument in this thread. Don't demand a cheerleader; demand evidence.","replies":[{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"Precisely. I want the trophies to stay in his notebook even on the days we don't play perfectly.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"Pio scored the winner at 75 after the post at 54. If the columnist needs a lesson in pressure, he can study that twenty-one-minute interval.","replies":[{"user":"PioNation","text":"A player being denied, staying ready and finishing the next chance. That's the lesson.","replies":[{"user":"CurvaB","text":"And it sent Barcelona home. That really happened.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"An achievement should be allowed to be an achievement before being turned into another exam.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"I don't mind hard questions. I mind refusing the obvious conclusion that this Napoli side has already done something extraordinary.","replies":[{"user":"BellandiReceipts","text":"It can still improve and already belong in conversations about great teams.","replies":[{"user":"OldSchoolNapoli","text":"History does not reset every Tuesday morning.","replies":[]}]}]},{"user":"JuveWatch","text":"Do you think Bellandi will finally be satisfied if Napoli win a third consecutive European Cup?","replies":[{"user":"SaladinoOutNow","text":"Absolutely not. Three would demonstrate an alarming lack of variety in competition selection.","replies":[{"user":"JuveWatch","text":"I walked straight into that trap.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"CurvaB","text":"I'd happily win the third just to test the theory.","replies":[]}]},{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"I'll read the tactics before the quarterfinal. Tonight the last word belongs to two crosses, Moore, and Pio. Those three decided the tie.","replies":[{"user":"BlaugranaView","text":"As a Barcelona supporter, I find that impossible to dispute.","replies":[{"user":"EuropeanNights","text":"For one evening, at least, we can agree on the ending.","replies":[]}]}]}]};for(const [id,threads] of Object.entries(additional)){const story=(D.articles||[]).find(a=>a.id===id);if(!story)throw Error('Barcelona article missing: '+id);story.comments=[...(story.comments||[]),...threads];}})();
